240:\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory $t$ interval across the six market means, [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], includes zero; the gap is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood. The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives $S$ from \site{rs_loo_lo} to \site{rs_loo_hi} over both search versions, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset} lists the gaps per market. The same holds for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 gain difference is \site{rs_cr1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_cr0} under D2 ($t$ interval [\site{rs_cr0_lo}, \site{rs_cr0_hi}]; doubled searches [\site{rs_cr0_dlo}, \site{rs_cr0_dhi}]). The difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, is \site{rs_i_b3} against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3, \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_fx_c} against C$^*$, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3}, \site{rs_rev_r3}, and \site{rs_rev_c}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. The truthful references can: \site{rs_ref_any} of the \site{rs_ref_n} frozen references replace an identity on the evaluation seeds. Each rule's reference cancels in $I$ but not in $G$. As a sensitivity calculation, we measured on the Mac each frozen reference's profit with replacement and with replacement disabled, and added the signed difference to the stored Linux gains; the adjustment can increase or decrease a gain, and it is not a re-evaluation with non-replacing references. The largest absolute difference is \site{rs_ref_max}; the adjustment moves G(D2+H3) and G(D2) by the same amount, less than \const{0.001}, leaves $S$ at \site{rs_ref_s}, and moves the C$^*$ minus R3 difference by less than \const{0.002}. The Mac reproduces the stored reference profits only on average, with a largest absolute mean discrepancy of about \site{rs_ref_val}, close to the largest replacement difference. $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}). \site{rs_flag} of the \site{rs_flag_n} D2 doubling checks are flagged (largest change \site{rs_flag_max}), and on fresh0 the D2 gap moves from \site{rs_f0_g0} to \site{rs_f0_g0_d} under doubling; an unflagged cell is not a converged one. In two frozen comparisons the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2 winner: $I$ is \site{rs_nest_c} for C$^*$ on fresh3 and \site{rs_nest_r} for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or differences between training and evaluation outcomes; they disappear under doubling, which does not establish convergence. We keep the frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.
243:\caption{Exploratory follow-up, after the report: the difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, per rule, its contrasts, the B3 minus R3 gaps $G$ under each family, and the share $S$, pooled over the six fresh markets with equal weights, with paired seed-bootstrap percentile intervals; frozen and doubled searches. $S$ is shown only when the lower bound of the pooled D2+H3 gap exceeds the preset floor. The lower block gives the gaps per market (D2+H3 / D2).}\label{tab:ph-reset}
246:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
250:\caption{Q2 under D2+H3: factorial contrasts, searched and fixed attacks, with attack and reference terms and efficiency (descriptive, nominal intervals).}\label{tab:q2}
255:\caption{Q2 under X7-SC, as in Table~\ref{tab:q2}.}\label{tab:q2-x7sc}
260:\caption{Q2: factorial contrasts with nominal intervals.}\label{fig:q2}
263:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
266:\caption{Exploratory, after the report: at the headline market, the efficiency of the selected W1 and W0 attacks, and, for each companion family, the episode-level lottery between the W0 attack and a profitable attack of the same rule (two displayed companions, H4 and D2+H3; all five examined companions are in \texttt{posthoc.json}) at the largest W0 weight on a grid of step \const{0.0005} whose mean gain keeps a non-negative nominal one-sided \const{95} percent lower bound, unadjusted for selection; estimated gain, that bound, and the mixture's efficiency, all on the evaluation records that selected the weight; ``Train gain'' is the mixture's mean gain on the training seeds (the registered W1 constraint requires it to be non-negative), reconstructed from the search archives. The row marked training-feasible, added after external review, uses the largest grid weight at which both the evaluation bound and the training gain are non-negative. B3's W0 attack has a positive estimated gain.}\label{tab:ph-welfare}
277:\caption{Q3 attack envelope per core rule and market; H4 at the headline market only.}\label{tab:q3-envelope}
287:\caption{Q3 coalitions: total and minimum-member gain against the coordinated truthful reference. The objectives are searched separately, so the two columns can describe different selected policies; the minimum column is not the minimum-member gain under the total-gain-selected policy.}\label{tab:q3-coalitions}
295:\paragraph{Secondary comparison.} At the headline market, with each rule's own tuned parameters, the gains are \site{r_sec_b3t} (B3-t), \site{r_sec_r3t} (R3-t), and \site{r_sec_cst} (C$^*$-t) under D2+H3. This comparison is secondary and descriptive.
297:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting comparison on the existing records (exploratory) shows evaluation discrepancies that call for further search checks, also in unflagged cells: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). Winners are selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds, and picking the better D2+H3 winner on the evaluation seeds favours it, so this is a discrepancy, not a demonstrated search failure. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
300:\caption{The Q1 differences from the report beside their re-evaluation with the registered fresh-market configurations (frozen policies and references).}\label{tab:ph-stability}
305:\caption{Q4 per market from the report (``report'') beside the re-evaluation with the registered fresh-market configurations (``reg.''): gain difference and efficiency with their calibrated lower bounds, the Holm-adjusted market value under the registered configurations, and both decisions (frozen policies and references).}\label{tab:ph-q4-stability}
309:\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The original numerical scoring path, preserved in the released scoring output, retains estimates from completed searches even when their doubling checks were marked incomplete; it counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. A separate eligibility analysis applies the two filters below. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official retrospective score. If a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and \site{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: \site{r_pred_qd_ful}, \site{r_pred_qd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_qd_na}). The broader sensitivity excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful} fulfilled, \site{r_pred_d_fail} failed, and \site{r_pred_d_na} not assessed (counting direct inputs only: \site{r_pred_dd_ful}, \site{r_pred_dd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_dd_na}). No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by less than one hundred-thousandth), so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).
313:\caption{Prediction tally under three eligibility readings, added after external review. Numerical: the scorer's statuses (Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b}). Flagged inputs, Q3: a Q3 cell with a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor is not assessed. Flagged inputs, all non-exempt cells: a flagged scored cell is excluded in every prediction except six decision-related predictions retained by convention; a prediction fails if any eligible cell fails. An inherited flag marks a potentially relevant ancestor, not a demonstrated change. The last two rows apply the same filters to the listed inputs of each cell only, without tracing. Eighteen scored predictions, including the alternative to Q1-a.}\label{tab:pred-eligibility}
318:\caption{Registered predictions for Q1 and Q2, scored from the report. Identifiers and statements as in the registered predictions file (supplement); a pair of values gives two statistics of the scored cell, as labelled, not an interval. Status as scored on the numerical estimates; $\dagger$: not assessed under both stricter eligibility readings of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility}; $\ddagger$: not assessed under the broader reading only.}\label{tab:predictions}
331:\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:results}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
333:\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
337:\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity marks a provider that has replaced; population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
341:For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
344:\paragraph{Availability.} The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, and \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
349:First test and headline market: providers $i = 1, \dots, 10$ have $q^{\max}_i = (0.60, 0.65, \dots, 1.00, 1.00)$, $b_i = (0.40, 0.38, \dots, 0.22)$, $a_i = 0.02$, $k_i = 6$, $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}] = [0.6, 1.4]$, $\sigma = 0.05$, $\beta = 0.05$, $\rho = 0.2$. Fresh markets of the second test: $N$, $J$, $T$, $\sigma$, $\beta$, $\rho$, and $v_{\min}$ as at the headline; $q^{\max}$ ten sorted draws from $U[0.55, 1.0]$; $b_i$ falling linearly in the quality rank between $b_{\mathrm{hi}} \sim U[0.34, 0.46]$ and $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \sim U[0.18, 0.26]$ with noise $U[-0.02, 0.02]$; $a_i \sim U[0, 0.05]$; $k_i$ uniform in $\{4, \dots, 8\}$; $v_{\max} \sim U[1.2, 1.6]$. The focal provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus $S^*$ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.
353:\caption{Development comparator selection (before the lock; development records): each credibility configuration's selected D2+H3 attack gain and gross efficiency at the inner search budget, and the two configurations rechecked at full budget, at the headline market and dev0, with R3's pacing and the fixed pair. Score: the development objective, the minimum over markets and families of the smaller of an efficiency margin and a gain margin, with the market and family that decide it; the efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration.}\label{tab:comparator-dev}
359:\paragraph{Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}.} Let $V_{\mathrm{clean}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{fail}}$ be the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one, so $C_U = V_{\mathrm{clean}} - V_{\mathrm{fail}}$. By the first condition, immediate free replacement attains the clean distribution of future states after a failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \ge V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. By the second, any plan after a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied after no failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \le V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. Hence $C_U = 0$. For B2 and B3 the second condition holds because a failed identity carries only its ban, with no priority or payment privilege, a clean provider can copy waiting by not offering and can itself replace its identity, no bond is lost, and the audit budget and rival states do not depend on the focal identity's history; replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation. Refunds of the failed jobs in the current round still enter Lemma~\ref{lem:local}. A rule that gave priority to an identity that waits out its ban would violate the second condition, and the corollary would not apply to it.
361:\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}.} R3's priority list is ordered by age group, newest first. Within a group, the audit keys are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables, so the group's jobs are exchangeable and the selected subset is uniform among them; delivery noise is independent of selection.

