 3. Run A: the runner-up comparator
 This tests whether the small Q1 ordering depends on the development objective. The paper already says: “C∗
 is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule...”
 (p. 6). A runner-up win would change that ordering, but would not erase the B3 reset interaction or prove that
 the runner-up is universally best.

 Fix the comparison before its outcomes are seen, label it exploratory, and use the same budgets and
 evaluation conventions. It is useful, but I expect a smaller score effect than honest churn or materially stronger
 optimization evidence. It is not a prerequisite for a submission that consistently retains the existing
 comparator-selection qualification.



