 (c) Interpretation
 The abstract’s sentence is supportable as a statement about pooled prescribed-search estimates, with one
 important wording change:

 “An exploratory follow-up after the report, specified after these outcomes were seen, finds that within the D2
 family most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches, and the C∗ minus R3
 difference, disappear when attacker replacement is disabled.” (p. 1.)

 The B3–R3 collapse is large relative to its original measured gap, survives doubling, and is not solely produced
 by one market. However, “disappear” should mean a much smaller pooled estimate, not a proved zero
 population effect or disappearance in every market. For example, the doubled D2 C∗−R3 difference on fresh5
 is −0.047035, despite its pooled value of −0.003466; these are my paired-mean calculations from the
 corresponding held-out arrays in records/reset/doubling/.

 S above one is legitimate arithmetic because its numerator gap is negative. It is not a percentage of an
 identified causal effect that somehow exceeds the whole. Two different uncertainty statements coexist: the
 seed-bootstrap interval excludes zero for the average D2 gap in these fixed markets, while the exploratory



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                                           5
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 interval across market means includes zero. The latter does not establish equivalence. The added
 leave-one-out analysis usefully limits an interpretation of the small negative pooled gap as a stable reversal.

 The reference sensitivity uses the correct signed algebra. Define v_r as the normalized profit of the original
 frozen reference minus the same reference policy with replacement disabled. Then changing the benchmark
 changes gain by v_r. Both B3–R3 gaps shift by v_B3−v_R3, while I_r is unchanged. Thus the interaction
 I(B3)−I(R3) is reference-free, although G and S are not. The small computed pooled adjustment supports the
 reported sensitivity; it does not validate transferring a Mac counterfactual difference into Linux gains as an
 exact replay. Nor does disabling a switch reoptimize the truthful reference within a no-replacement policy
 class.

 I would retain the sensitivity with those limits. A complete resolution would replay both frozen-reference
 policies on the recorded Linux environment and, only if a different optimized-benchmark estimand is intended,
 select a separate non-replacing truthful reference under a newly fixed procedure. That additional computation
 is not required merely to report the current descriptive interaction honestly.


 (d) Contribution
 This follow-up strengthens the mechanism account, rather than changing any registered decision. The original
 comparison measured a rule difference with replacement available. The new comparison measures how that
 difference changes when the D2 attacker lacks replacement, while fixed-policy interventions show the switch
 matters substantially more to the selected B3 attacker than to the selected R3 attacker.

 The supported contribution is an interaction between audit design and the prescribed attacker family. It does
 not establish that age targeting uniquely causes the improvement, that R3 is optimal, or that the result
 survives competition from benign newcomers. A natural interpretation is that replacement-sensitive targeting
 restores some enforcement pressure that uniform auditing loses when bans can be evaded. C∗ is another
 such design.

 This distinction matters for novelty. Cheap-pseudonym newcomer costs and credibility-based checking with
 rejoining are established subjects, as the paper acknowledges. I verified primary sources for Friedman and
 Resnick’s The Social Cost of Cheap Pseudonyms and Sarmenta’s Sabotage-Tolerance Mechanisms for
 Volunteer Computing Systems. The contribution must therefore come from the controlled budgeted
 comparison, decomposition, and measured boundaries, not the general idea of scrutinizing newcomers. Stable
 sources: https://presnick.people.si.umich.edu/papers/identifiers/ and
 https://groups.csail.mit.edu/cag/bayanihan/papers/ccgrid01/ccgrid01.pdf.



 5. New or remaining problems, ranked by severity

 1. A held-out ordering reversal is misclassified, then used to favor an analysis
 Exact text: “With the frozen searches, two cells violate the nesting of D2 in D2+H3: I is −0.002 for C∗ on
 fresh3 and −0.006 for R3 on fresh4. The doubled searches remove both, so for the C∗ contrast the doubled
 value is the more reliable one.” (p. 16.)

 Nesting orders optimal values under a common objective. It does not order held-out outcomes of different
 training-selected policies. The paper itself gives the correct qualification for X7-SC on p. 19: “Winners are
 selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds...” The two implicated D2+H3
 cells independently have doubling flags, so search sensitivity is real. That still does not make restoration of the
 expected held-out signs a criterion for choosing the more reliable estimate.

 Exact fix, text only: “In two frozen comparisons, the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2
 winner: I is −0.002 for C∗ on fresh3 and −0.006 for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or
 differences between training and evaluation outcomes. They disappear under doubling, which does not
 establish convergence. We retain frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.”

 Analysis on existing records: with the original search archives supplied, compare recorded training-objective
 values of the embedded D2 candidate and selected D2+H3 candidate under a common objective. If that
 comparison requires fresh simulator calls, it needs compute. Rename nesting_failures to



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                          6
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 heldout_order_reversals, or explicitly document the existing field as a limited diagnostic. I reject the earlier
 referee’s stronger inference here.


 2. The abstract claims a search-error comparison that was never established
 Exact text: “Second, R3’s two targeting parts substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the
 pooled reduction in the attacker’s gain, and in R3’s own comparison (with and without the priority window,
 under D2+H3) the window changes the gain by −0.003, which is within search error.” (p. 1.)

 The number is a measured contrast. “Within search error” implies an error assessment for that contrast, but
 the fresh F011 factorial cells have no scheduled doubling check. Observed optimizer improvements elsewhere,
 or the flagging threshold, do not supply its uncertainty bound. The paper explicitly says the relevant narrow
 intervals measure seed noise only (p. 19).

 Exact fix, text only: “In R3’s own comparison, the priority window changes the pooled measured gain by only
 −0.003 under D2+H3, but search uncertainty for this contrast is unquantified.”

 Needs compute only if the stronger claim is retained: extend and replicate both sides of that specific search
 contrast under a declared procedure. Even then, observed sensitivity is not an optimality bound.


 3. “Disappears” and “not distinguishable” need explicit aggregation and uncertainty
 targets
 Exact text: the abstract’s disappearance sentence quoted in section 4(c), and “...but that gap is not
 distinguishable from zero...” (p. 15).

 The paper now provides the necessary evidence, but the phrasing allows readers to equate a small pooled
 estimate or a non-rejection with a zero effect. It also leaves the seed-level versus market-level distinction
 implicit at the critical sentence.

 Exact abstract fix, text only: “An exploratory D2-family follow-up, specified after the registered outcomes were
 seen, reduces the pooled B3 minus R3 gap from 0.173 to −0.009 and the pooled C∗ minus R3 gap from 0.021
 to −0.003 when attacker replacement is disabled, using the prescribed searches and unchanged references.”

 Exact p. 15 fix, text only: “The pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero
 conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory interval across market means
 includes zero. We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood.”

 The replacement numbers are the rounded regenerated values in section 4(b), not new estimates selected for
 this review.


 4. Disabling replacement does not always reduce a frozen reference’s profit
 Exact text: “As a sensitivity calculation, we measured on the Mac how much replacement is worth to each
 reference and added it to the stored Linux gains (a reference without replacement earns less, so the gain
 against it rises); this is not a re-evaluation with non-replacing references.” (p. 15.)

 Three stored signed effects contradict this: fresh2 B3, −0.000352451; fresh4 C∗, −0.005938855; fresh5 C∗,
 −0.000629950 (RR.cells.*.replacement_value_to_reference). In those stored evaluations, disabling
 replacement raises the estimated profits of the corresponding frozen reference policies. The code uses the
 signed values correctly, so this is a textual sign error, not an error in the pooled adjustment.

 Exact fix, text only: “We add the signed difference between each frozen reference’s profit with replacement
 and with replacement disabled to its stored gains; this adjustment can increase or decrease the gain.”

 Also replace “to within 0.007” with “with a maximum absolute mean discrepancy of approximately 0.0074,”
 from RR.summary.max_abs_validation_mean_diff. Retain the explicit cross-platform caveat. This check
 measures a fixed-policy difference, not a nonnegative optimized option value.




Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                       7
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 5. The process account understates new evaluations, and the package cannot
 establish all historical claims
 The paper’s exact timing sentence is quoted in row 7 of the follow-through table (p. 14). Exact response text:
 “Every change below is text, or an analysis on existing records.”

 The added ledger resolves the missing-documentation complaint, but the latter sentence is false for the
 reference simulations. Also, an analysis-code commit after records arrived is not evidence that the analysis
 implementation was frozen before outcomes existed. The paper correctly claims pre-result specification of the
 plan, not of every later analytic addition; keep that distinction.

 Exact fix, text only: use the corrected response opening in section 3; explicitly identify the reference
 simulations and review_r3 diagnostics as later exploratory work. Analysis on existing records: include the
 relevant signed commit/history evidence and omitted headline diagnostic summaries in the eventual artifact. I
 am not alleging undisclosed changes or treating the disclosed package omissions as misconduct.

 I found no new numerical contradiction in the checked registered summaries. Independent checks reproduce
 the Q1 D2+H3 mean difference, 0.020720, and its market-level interval [−0.012789, 0.054229] from the
 evaluation records; the Q1 Holm values, 0.0008 for each family, and Q4 adjusted values, 0.0024 for the
 passing markets, from records/report.json; and the lottery calculations from the supplied CSVs. The latter
 remain selected on evaluation data, as the manuscript now explicitly states. These checks verify arithmetic
 and bookkeeping, not nominal coverage or complete search adequacy.



 6. Open runs, ranked by potential score change

 1. Honest-churn arm
 This has the greatest potential to change my WEIS score. The exact limitation is: “The honest opponents never
 replace their identities...” (p. 27). Contrary to the previous referee’s characterization, not every newcomer is
 dishonest: truthful focal references can replace. The structural problem is that replacement activity after
 initialization is concentrated in the focal provider. It can remain newest without ordinary benign identities
 competing for audit priority.

 A useful arm would compare B3, R3, and C∗ at prespecified nonzero churn rates on the same fresh markets,
 hold the population model clear, and jointly report attacker gain, honest newcomer profit/refunds, audit
 allocation, and efficiency. It should search attacks under the churn environment, not merely replay policies
 optimized without it. A negative result can strengthen the paper by identifying a failure boundary; a run does
 not automatically earn a higher score merely by existing.

 Before WEIS submission? No listed run is mandatory for submitting this narrowly scoped paper. Honest churn
 is the experiment I would prioritize to move my WEIS score and would require before supporting a broader
 policy-comparison claim. It is not logically necessary to support the existing restricted finite-search claims. I
 therefore reject the earlier review’s unconditional prerequisite, while agreeing that honest churn has the
 greatest scientific value.


 2. Run B: stronger searches with explicit stopping and nesting controls
 This matters for WEIS as well as AAMAS. The substantive conclusions concern attacker opportunities, even
 though the labels are carefully restricted to prescribed searches. Specify a larger fixed budget, independent
 optimizer starts, inclusion of existing candidates under the same training objective, and an empirical stopping
 rule. “To convergence” must not imply a certified global optimum.

 Prioritize flagged core cells, fresh5 B3 under D2, and the F111−F011 contrast if it remains in the abstract.
 Preserve training/evaluation separation; do not choose a favored search version because its held-out signs fit
 the mechanism story. Use fresh evaluation data for any new inferential assessment of selected policies.

 This would be my highest-priority change for AAMAS. A full unconstrained search campaign is not a
 prerequisite to submit an honestly scoped WEIS study, but weak optimization can affect its economic
 interpretation.



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                        8
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 3. Run A: the runner-up comparator
 This tests whether the small Q1 ordering depends on the development objective. The paper already says: “C∗
 is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule...”
 (p. 6). A runner-up win would change that ordering, but would not erase the B3 reset interaction or prove that
 the runner-up is universally best.

 Fix the comparison before its outcomes are seen, label it exploratory, and use the same budgets and
 evaluation conventions. It is useful, but I expect a smaller score effect than honest churn or materially stronger
 optimization evidence. It is not a prerequisite for a submission that consistently retains the existing
 comparator-selection qualification.



 7. Decision
 These acceptance probabilities are my subjective editorial forecasts, not estimates from venue acceptance
 statistics. Confidence in the reproduced arithmetic is high; confidence in venue outcomes is lower. Scores use
 the requested five-point scale, with the middle score representing a borderline recommendation.

  Venue                   Score        Acceptance      Confidence         Single change that would move my score most
                                       probability


  WEIS 2027               2/5, near    30%             Medium             A prespecified honest-churn experiment showing the operating
                          borderline                                      region and honest-newcomer cost of the security benefit,
                                                                          followed by a focused presentation of that result.


  AAMAS main track        2/5          12%             Medium             A substantially stronger adaptive-search evaluation with nested
                                                                          training-candidate controls and explicit search sensitivity for
                                                                          the principal contrasts.


  ACM EC                  1/5          3%              Medium-high on     A substantive new mechanism-design or equilibrium result
                                                       contribution fit   connecting audit capacity, identity replacement, and newcomer
                                                                          costs. The listed simulation runs alone are unlikely to supply
                                                                          that contribution.


 I would preserve the registered failures, exploratory qualifications, and unchanged historical results. The next
 revision should make the mechanism comparison more informative and the account shorter, while correcting
 the specific inferential and sign claims identified above.




