efficiency comparison under D2+H3 passes on 3 of the six markets (Table 10), and all three passes
(fresh1, fresh3, fresh4) are about the prescribed search procedure. On every market the estimated
B3 minus R3 gain difference is positive and R3 keeps its efficiency condition; where the comparison
fails, the difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: 0.041 on fresh0, 0.057 on
fresh2, and 0.027 on fresh5, against 0.469 on fresh1. R3’s efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is 0.985.
The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh0
and fresh3 through their references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing
markets failed. Q4 does not test the complete amended claim of the first test. Table 9 (exploratory)
decomposes each market’s gain difference, which is mostly attacked profit. R3’s reported audit
spending is about five percent of the realised first-best surplus, and the market’s service fraction
(all jobs served over the first-best number) is near one. The three non-passing markets have smaller
attacked-profit reductions; these descriptive diagnostics do not identify their cause.

Identity replacement and the B3–R3 gap (exploratory follow-up). D2+H3 is D2 plus one
switch x that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the
headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves 0.307 more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and
0.012 more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is 0.302 under D2+H3 and 0.007 under D2. To
see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget
cut 3 had removed (B3, R3, and C∗ at ρ = 0.2, each with a doubling check), under a plan and
